{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-6657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x-1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-6657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-1$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-1}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-6657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55d74e409d644c2db25679c2832e208478fa37ef7957daf84df23fffdbadec20"} {"question" "q-6657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-6657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-6657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-83864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-14x-24$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,8),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-83864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-24$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-24}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 12,\\,\\pm 24\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(3,8),$ are $4$ and $6.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-14$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $20$ | $24$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+5x+6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+5x+6) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=4,-2,-3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n4-2-3=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-83864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55d74e409d644c2db25679c2832e208478fa37ef7957daf84df23fffdbadec20"} {"question" "q-83864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-83864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-83864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-112873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-4x+5?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-112873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $5$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }5}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{6}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(6)$ is not a factor of $8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-112873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55d74e409d644c2db25679c2832e208478fa37ef7957daf84df23fffdbadec20"} {"question" "q-112873" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-112873" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-112873" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-275954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+20x-12$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}).$ In **ascending order**, the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-275954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-12$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-12}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $20$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-8$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-8x+8) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(2x-3){(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$  and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-275954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55d74e409d644c2db25679c2832e208478fa37ef7957daf84df23fffdbadec20"} {"question" "q-275954" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-275954" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-275954" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-275954" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-321645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3x+2$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-321645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4$, and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ } \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $10$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 5=10.$)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-321645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "55d74e409d644c2db25679c2832e208478fa37ef7957daf84df23fffdbadec20"} {"question" "q-321645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"} {"question" "q-321645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068154/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1b256a57a0dab1756fea8ce73fc0c2b8114b1411f51e264c7beef2a28a56a7a"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2281 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
